  "id": "Compound::DB00203 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Sildenafil, a phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor, is shown to bind to Cyp2e1 and Cyp3a4, two cytochrome P450 enzymes, which are involved in the metabolism of various substrates. The evidence suggests that Sildenafil may indirectly influence Cyp2d6 through its interaction with Cyp2e1 and Cyp3a4, which in turn participate in the metabolism of substrates that are subsequently processed by Cyp2d6. The shared involvement of Cyp2d6 in the metabolism of arachidonic acid derivatives, as indicated by the participation of Cyp2e1 and Cyp3a4, implies a potential mechanistic link between Sildenafil and Cyp2d6. The final scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, supported by the consistent interaction of Sildenafil with Cyp2e1 and Cyp3a4, which are both involved in the metabolic pathways of substrates that are processed by Cyp2d6. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Sildenafil in modulating the activity of Cyp2d6 through its influence on the expression or function of upstream enzymes.",
